call_details.response:
The board configuration shows a standard Connect Four position in which Black (x) has three pieces and White (o) has two. No terminal threats exist on the present turn: White cannot complete four-in-a-row with any single drop, and Black likewise cannot complete four-in-a-row on the subsequent turn.

A one-ply evaluation that scores every open four-window (1 point for a single friendly piece, 5 points for two friendly pieces, dead windows worth 0) immediately ranks the legal drops:

- column 3 yields the highest differential (+13 for White, -2 for Black);
- column 2 is second;
- columns 0/1 are third;
- the edge columns trail badly.

The same ranking survives a forced-move-aware three-ply examination. After White’s drop in column 3 the principal replies available to Black are:

- the bottom-left extension that produces a three-in-a-row (forcing White to the edge), after which White’s recapture restores a comfortable positive score;
- the direct vertical block at (2,3). White’s strongest answer is then the adjacent drop at (3,4), again leaving a positive score of 9.

Every alternative first move for White permits Black to force a strictly lower residual score. Consequently the unique maximising action is the central continuation that both augments White’s own vertical and diagonal windows and simultaneously extinguishes two of Black’s most dangerous diagonals.

Final Answer: 3

